          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-highest-means" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Highest Protein Means</a>
          <a href="#how-we-ranked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Ranked Them</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#scoop-inflation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Why Big Scoops Inflate the Number</a>
          <a href="#per-calorie" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Protein per Calorie</a>
          <a href="#isolate-concentrate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Isolate vs Concentrate</a>
          <a href="#premium-labels" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Hydrolyzed, Clear and Other Labels</a>
          <a href="#how-much-per-scoop" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. How Much per Scoop You Need</a>
          <a href="#plant-density" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Plant Protein Density</a>
          <a href="#gainers-blends" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Gainers and High-Protein Blends</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The biggest number on the front of a tub is not the same thing as the most protein. A 30 g claim can come from a dense isolate or from a scoop half again as large as everyone else's. The powders that genuinely lead on protein deliver the most per gram of powder and per calorie, and this guide ranks six of them on exactly those measures, then shows you how to check any product yourself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-highest-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Highest Protein: Per Scoop, per Calorie or per Gram?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three honest ways to measure how high in protein a powder is, and they give different answers. Protein per scoop is the number brands print largest, and it is the least informative on its own, because a scoop is whatever size the brand decides. Protein per calorie tells you how much of the energy in a shake comes from protein rather than carbohydrate and fat. Protein by weight tells you how much of the powder you are paying for is actually protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each measure answers a different question. If you want the fewest scoops to hit a daily target, protein per scoop matters. If you are dieting and every calorie counts, protein per calorie is the deciding number. If you want to know whether a product is padded with flavoring, sweeteners, thickeners or carbohydrate, protein by weight is the cleanest test, and it is the one that exposes oversized scoops.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best high protein supplement powders score well on all three at once. A plain whey isolate typically delivers 25 g or more per scoop, gets close to 90 percent of its calories from protein and is somewhere between 80 and 90 percent protein by weight. A typical flavored whey blend lands closer to 24 g per scoop, 80 percent protein by calories and 75 to 80 percent by weight. A mass gainer can have 50 g of protein per serving and still be one of the lowest-density products in the store.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when we call a powder the highest protein powder, we mean it ranks near the top on density, not just on the headline dose. A product that hits 30 g per serving by using a 45 g scoop is not higher in protein than one that hits 25 g with a 28 g scoop; it simply gives you more powder. The rest of this guide shows you how to tell the difference in a few seconds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Ranked Protein Density
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not send products to a lab or run our own taste tests for this guide. The rankings come from label analysis, published certification status and the research on protein dosing, the method laid out on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Any comments on taste or mixing reflect what buyers widely report, not a claim of hands-on testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For every candidate we took the protein per serving, the serving weight in grams and the calories from the label, then calculated two ratios: protein as a percentage of serving weight, and protein calories as a percentage of total calories, using four calories per gram of protein. Products had to deliver at least 24 g of protein per serving, at least roughly three-quarters of their weight as protein and the large majority of their calories from protein to be considered.
          </p>

          <p>
            We then screened for honesty. We excluded proprietary blends that hide the protein sources, and products listing free amino acids such as glycine or taurine in a way that could inflate a nitrogen-based protein figure, a practice known as amino spiking. We gave credit for independent certifications such as Informed Choice and for brands that publish third-party lab results, because a high protein claim is only valuable if it is true.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we weighed practicality. A product that wins on paper but that buyers consistently say is hard to drink did not make the cut, because the most protein-dense powder in the world does nothing in the cupboard. We kept one plant option so that dairy-free readers have a genuinely dense choice, and we included one high single-scoop product so you can see how dose and density differ. If cost per gram matters more than density, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder guide</a> ranks budget options on cost per 25 g instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Highest Protein Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pick below delivers at least 24 g of protein per serving, gets roughly three-quarters or more of its weight from protein, and gets the large majority of its calories from protein. They are ranked on a balance of protein per calorie, protein share by weight and dose per scoop. Figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the panel on the tub you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
