        <div id="scoop-inflation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Bigger Scoops Inflate the Headline Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein per serving is a function of two things: how dense the powder is and how much powder is in a serving. Brands control the second number completely. A product that is 70 percent protein by weight will show 21 g of protein in a 30 g scoop and 31.5 g in a 45 g scoop. Same powder, same quality, 50 percent bigger headline. Nothing on the front of the tub tells you which version you are looking at.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the serving weight on the nutrition panel is the most important number most people never read. Divide protein per serving by serving weight and you get protein by weight, a figure that cannot be gamed by changing the scoop. Plain isolates and unflavored single-ingredient powders tend to land between about 83 and 90 percent. Flavored isolates often sit in the high 70s or low 80s, flavored blends in the mid to high 70s, and anything below about 70 percent is carrying a lot of non-protein material per scoop.
          </p>

