            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate protein percentage by weight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the grams of protein per serving by the serving weight in grams, both from the nutrition panel, and multiply by 100. A 25 g dose in a 28 g scoop is about 89 percent protein. Plain isolates usually land in the mid to high 80s; flavored blends in the mid to high 70s.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey isolate higher in protein than concentrate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Isolate is at least 90 percent protein on a dry basis, while sports concentrates are commonly around 80 percent. On a finished label that typically means about 85 to 90 percent protein by weight for plain isolate versus the 70s for flavored concentrate. For muscle gain the two perform similarly when the dose is matched.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 30 g of protein per scoop better than 25 g?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. It depends on whether the extra protein comes from a denser powder or a bigger scoop, and on your needs. Most adults do well with 20 to 40 g per meal. A larger dose is convenient for big lifters, but total daily protein matters far more than the size of a single shake.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which plant protein powder is highest in protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unflavored pea isolates tend to be the densest. Naked Pea lists about 27 g of protein in a 30 g serving, roughly 90 percent by weight, per the label. Pea is lower in methionine and leucine than whey, so a slightly bigger serving or a pea and rice blend helps close the gap.
              </p>
